Rules the code enforces
Money is integer minor units. Rates are basis points. Rounding is half-up per line, so lines always add up to the total.
No oversell. Every stock change is one guarded UPDATE. The database also has CHECK constraints. A concurrency test with 12 threads and 5 units proves it.
Append-only ledger. Triggers block UPDATE and DELETE on inventory_ledger.
Idempotency. Every mutating call takes a key (order ids are the key for place_order). A retry returns the stored first result. The same key with a different request raises IdempotencyConflict. Failed calls store nothing and can be retried.
Atomic. Each operation is one transaction. A failure in any step, such as the stock reservation, rolls back the whole operation.
State guards. Every status change is a WHERE status IN (...) update. A stale or repeated request raises InvalidTransition.
Prices come from the catalog. The caller sends SKUs and quantities only.
Refunds add up. Partial returns use cumulative rounding, so the sum of all refunds for an order equals what the customer paid.
Cash needs an open shift. Expected cash is opening float plus cash sales minus cash refunds.
Refund flow
cancel and return_items create refund rows. Cash refunds are confirmed at once. Card refunds are pending. Your code sends each pending refund to the payment provider, then calls payments.confirm_refund(payment_id, provider_ref). The provider call stays outside the database transaction on purpose.
